<.page title="Nodes" description="How Edge Agent turns a machine into an Edge Admin node.">
  <article class="guide-content guide-topic">
    <a class="back-link" href="/">← Guide overview</a>
    <p class="kicker">Concept 02</p><h1>Nodes are the machines you operate.</h1>
    <p class="lede">A node is a machine running Edge Agent in a cluster. It is addressed through the cluster mesh, not through a public IP or host SSH port.</p>
    <section><h2>Enrollment is the handoff</h2><p>An enrollment key joins Edge Agent to a cluster's WireGuard mesh. The agent then registers the node with Edge Admin and receives the identity and credentials it needs for ongoing communication.</p><p>From that point, Edge Admin tracks the node's health and can use direct VPN delivery with HTTP fallback when conditions require it.</p></section>
    <section><h2>A node is more than a record</h2><p>It is the endpoint for commands, metrics, diagnostics, aliases, centralized SSH credentials, and network access through the proxy. Those capabilities are attached to a managed machine; the node is the common handle for all of them.</p></section>
    <section class="next"><h2>Use it</h2><p>Start by listing nodes in Swagger after an agent enrolls. Node detail tells you its cluster, health, identity, and the paths available to operate it.</p><div class="next__links"><a class="primary-link" href="/swaggerui#/Nodes.Node">Node API <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a href="/guide/commands">Next: commands</a></div></section>
  </article>
</.page>
